\title{Exact exponential heat rectification from the Liouvillian skin effect}

\author{Hikaru Wakaura}
\altaffiliation{ORCID: 0000-0001-8381-8323}
\email{h.wakaura@qiri.co.jp}
\affiliation{QIRI (Quantum Integrated Research Institute Inc.), Tokyo 107-0061, Japan}
\author{Taiki Tanimae}
\email{t.tanimae@qiri.co.jp}
\affiliation{QIRI (Quantum Integrated Research Institute Inc.), Tokyo 107-0061, Japan}

\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}
Thermal diodes realised so far rely on nonlinearity or structural asymmetry, and their rectification ratios do not grow with device size.
Non-Hermitian lattices with a skin effect transport excitations preferentially in one direction, but whether this bias survives in heat transport between thermal reservoirs, what sets its magnitude and what it costs thermodynamically have remained open.
We study a reciprocal tight-binding chain with directional bond dissipation between two thermal leads and show that the ratio of the forward and reverse heat-current responses obeys the exact law $\dQ_f/\dQ_r=\exp[2(L-1)/\xi]$, where $L$ is the chain length and $\xi$ the Liouvillian skin localisation length.
The law follows from an identity of tridiagonal Green functions; it holds at every energy and temperature, for fermionic and bosonic carriers, and for arbitrary on-site disorder, while bond disorder obeys an additive sum rule.
With next-nearest-neighbour hopping the exponent becomes the energy-resolved non-Bloch localisation length, although the spectral winding number vanishes at all transport energies.
Superfermion matrix-product-state simulations calibrated against the exact solution show that a nearest-neighbour interaction up to twice the hopping leaves $\xi$ unrenormalised and changes only a length-independent prefactor.
Replacing the loss by a finite-temperature bath closes the entropy balance: the ratio is independent of the bath state, and the entropy produced per unit of rectified heat grows as $1/\xi$.
We give parameters for cold-atom momentum lattices and superconducting circuits with engineered loss.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Directing heat is as fundamental to thermal engineering as directing charge is to electronics, and a thermal diode is the elementary building block of thermal logic and of heat management in nanoscale devices~\cite{Terraneo2002,Li2004,Chang2006,Li2012,Benenti2017}.
All thermal rectifiers demonstrated so far share one requirement: the two directions of heat flow must probe different energy spectra, which is achieved through anharmonicity, temperature-dependent spectral overlap or structural asymmetry~\cite{Segal2005,Li2012}, and the quantum thermal diodes proposed recently are no exception~\cite{Zhang2025}.
Because these mechanisms act locally, the resulting rectification ratios saturate at values of order one to ten and do not improve with the size of the device.

A qualitatively different source of directionality has emerged in non-Hermitian physics.
Lattices with asymmetric hopping~\cite{Hatano1996} pile all their eigenstates up at one edge, the non-Hermitian skin effect~\cite{Yao2018,Gong2018,Lee2019,Okuma2020,Bergholtz2021}, and the same phenomenon appears in the Liouvillian of an open quantum system whose dissipators carry a direction~\cite{Song2019,Haga2021,Metelmann2015,Rassaert2025,Shigedomi2026}, a directionality that survives dephasing~\cite{Wang2026}.
Skin modes have been observed in photonic, electrical and cold-atom platforms~\cite{Weidemann2020,Helbig2020,Xiao2020,Liang2022,Zhou2025}, and amplification along a skin lattice has been proposed for sensing~\cite{McDonald2020} and observed in bosonic Kitaev chains~\cite{Busnaina2024,Slim2024}.
Several works have connected non-Hermitian directionality with heat: a diffusive skin effect has been predicted and observed in classical thermal metamaterials~\cite{Cao2021,Liu2024}, which concerns heat conduction rather than quantum transport between reservoirs; thermal noise can be routed nonreciprocally through few-mode cascaded networks~\cite{Xuereb2018}, an effect that does not scale with system size; and the transport theory of non-Hermitian nonreciprocal systems has established conditions for reflectionless and transparent transport~\cite{Ghaemi2021}.
However, it is not known whether directional dissipation alone can rectify heat between two thermal reservoirs, what quantity controls the ratio, whether the effect survives interactions and disorder, and what entropy it produces.

In this paper we answer these questions for the simplest model, a reciprocal fermionic chain with directional bond loss between two leads, which is a boundary-driven open quantum system~\cite{Landi2022} with an additional bulk dissipator.
We find that the ratio of the heat-current responses in the two directions obeys an exact law, $\dQ_f/\dQ_r=e^{2(L-1)/\xi}$ with $\xi$ the skin length, for any energy, temperature and on-site disorder; that the controlling quantity beyond nearest neighbours is the energy-resolved non-Bloch localisation length and not a spectral winding number; that a nearest-neighbour interaction leaves $\xi$ unrenormalised; and that the entropic cost per rectified heat grows as $1/\xi$ when the dissipator is a finite-temperature bath.
The result shows that skin-effect rectification is a structural property of one-dimensional dissipative transport and provides a design rule, $L/\xi\gtrsim3$, for near-perfect heat diodes.

The paper is organised as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:model} defines the model, the Gaussian solution, the leads and the rectification measure.
Section~\ref{sec:law} proves the exact law and discusses its immediate consequences.
Section~\ref{sec:nonbloch} treats next-nearest-neighbour hopping and identifies the non-Bloch exponent.
Section~\ref{sec:int} presents the matrix-product-state method, its precision and the results for the interacting chain.
Section~\ref{sec:thermo} introduces the finite-temperature bulk bath and the entropy balance.
Section~\ref{sec:disorder} covers on-site and bond disorder, Sec.~\ref{sec:exp} the experimental parameters, and Sec.~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.
We set $\hbar=k_B=1$ and use $T_h=J$, $T_c=0.5J$ and lead chemical potential $\mu=0$ unless stated otherwise.

