\section{Model and formalism}
\label{sec:model}

\subsection{Chain with directional bond dissipation}

We consider spinless fermions on an open chain of $L$ sites, $H=-J\sum_{j=1}^{L-1}(c_j^\dagger c_{j+1}+{\rm h.c.})$, whose density matrix obeys the Lindblad equation $\dot\rho=-i[H,\rho]+\sum_a\mathcal{D}[L_a]\rho$ with one jump operator per bond,
\begin{equation}
L_j=\sqrt{\kappa}\,\bigl(c_j+e^{i\phi}c_{j+1}\bigr),\qquad j=1,\dots,L-1 ,
\label{eq:Lj}
\end{equation}
in addition to the jump operators of the leads [Fig.~\ref{fig1}(a)].
The Hamiltonian is reciprocal; the phase $\phi$ in the dissipator is the only source of directionality.
Equation~(\ref{eq:Lj}) is the chiral-damping dissipator of Song, Yao and Wang~\cite{Song2019} and the standard route to the Liouvillian skin effect~\cite{Haga2021}; it is obtained by coupling two neighbouring sites to a common strongly damped auxiliary mode with a relative coupling phase and eliminating that mode adiabatically~\cite{Metelmann2015}.
Site 1 (site $L$) is coupled to a fermionic lead at temperature $T_L$ ($T_R$) and chemical potential $\mu$.

\subsection{Gaussian steady state and effective non-Hermitian matrix}

Without interactions the Lindbladian is quadratic: $H=\sum_{mn}h_{mn}c_m^\dagger c_n$ and every jump operator is linear, $L_a=\sum_m l_{a,m}c_m$ (loss) or $L_a=\sum_m g_{a,m}c_m^\dagger$ (gain).
The two-point function $C_{mn}=\langle c_m^\dagger c_n\rangle$ then obeys the closed equation
\begin{equation}
\dot C=-(XC+CX^\dagger)+M_g^{T},\qquad X=\tfrac12(M_l+M_g^{T})-ih^{T},
\label{eq:lyap}
\end{equation}
with $M_l=\sum_a l_al_a^\dagger$ and $M_g=\sum_a g_ag_a^\dagger$, whose steady state solves the Lyapunov equation $XC+CX^\dagger=M_g^{T}$.
We solve it with a Bartels--Stewart solver and have verified the solution against the exact steady state of the full many-body Lindbladian on four modes to $10^{-8}$.
The energy and particle currents from reservoir $\alpha$ into the system follow from the jump operators that belong to $\alpha$ and from $h$ and $C$.
Single-particle amplitudes propagate with the non-Hermitian matrix $\heff=h-\tfrac{i}{2}(M_l^{T}+M_g)$, which for the bond loss of Eq.~(\ref{eq:Lj}) reads
\begin{equation}
\heff=h-\tfrac{i}{2}\textstyle\sum_j \bar l_j\, l_j^{T},\qquad
t_\pm=-J-\tfrac{i\kappa}{2}e^{\pm i\phi},
\label{eq:heff}
\end{equation}
where $l_j$ is the coefficient vector of $L_j$ and $t_+\equiv(\heff)_{j,j+1}$, $t_-\equiv(\heff)_{j+1,j}$ are the two hopping amplitudes.
The on-site part of $\heff$ is a uniform loss $-i\kappa$ ($-i\kappa/2$ on the two end sites), and the two hopping amplitudes differ in modulus, $|t_\mp|^2=(J\pm\tfrac{\kappa}{2}\sin\phi)^2+\tfrac{\kappa^2}{4}\cos^2\phi$.
For $\phi=\pi/2$, $\heff$ is the Hatano--Nelson chain~\cite{Hatano1996} with real asymmetric hoppings $-J\mp\kappa/2$ and a uniform loss; all its right eigenvectors are localised at the right edge with inverse localisation length
\begin{equation}
\xi^{-1}=\ln\Bigl|\frac{t_-}{t_+}\Bigr|\simeq\frac{\kappa\sin\phi}{J}\quad(\kappa\ll J).
\label{eq:xi}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Leads}

Two implementations of the leads are used.
(i) \emph{Wide-band leads}: the end sites acquire a self-energy $-i\Gamma/2$ and the heat currents are given by Landauer formulas with the transmissions
\begin{equation}
T_{RL}(\varepsilon)=\Gamma^2|G_{L1}(\varepsilon)|^2,\qquad T_{LR}(\varepsilon)=\Gamma^2|G_{1L}(\varepsilon)|^2,
\label{eq:T}
\end{equation}
where $G(\varepsilon)=(\varepsilon-\heff)^{-1}$ and $T_{RL}$ ($T_{LR}$) describes transport from the left lead to the right lead (from right to left); we use $\Gamma=J$ and integrate over $|\varepsilon|\le4J$.
(ii) \emph{Mesoscopic leads}: $K$ modes equally spaced in $[-W,W]$, each coupled to its end site with amplitude $v=\sqrt{\Gamma\Delta\varepsilon/2\pi}$ and thermalised by loss and gain at rates $\gamma(1-f)$ and $\gamma f$, with $f$ the Fermi function at $(T_\alpha,\mu)$ and $\gamma=\Delta\varepsilon$ the mode spacing.
The lead modes are part of the quadratic Lindbladian, so Eq.~(\ref{eq:lyap}) applies unchanged, and the construction carries over to the interacting chain of Sec.~\ref{sec:int}.
We use $K=10$ and $W=4J$ for the matrix-product-state runs and $K=10$--$80$ for checks.
The heat-current responses of the two implementations agree within $5$--$15\%$, the difference being a bandwidth effect.

\subsection{Rectification measure}

Rectification must be defined with care in a system that also loses energy to the bulk dissipator.
The total heat current absorbed by the cold lead contains a contribution that does not depend on the temperature of the other lead, because both leads lose energy to the loss channel.
If rectification is defined from total currents, this background dominates and the apparent rectification decays as $1/L$ for any $\kappa$, although the device is perfectly directional.
We therefore use the response of the cold lead to heating the other lead,
\begin{align}
\dQ_f&=\dot E_R(T_L{=}T_h,T_R{=}T_c)-\dot E_R(T_c,T_c),\nonumber\\
\dQ_r&=\dot E_L(T_L{=}T_c,T_R{=}T_h)-\dot E_L(T_c,T_c),
\label{eq:dQ}
\end{align}
where $\dot E_\alpha$ is the energy absorbed per unit time by lead $\alpha$: $\dQ_f$ is the additional heat absorbed by the right lead when the left lead is heated from $T_c$ to $T_h$, and $\dQ_r$ the additional heat absorbed by the left lead when the right lead is heated.
These differences remove the background exactly.
We quote the ratio $\dQ_f/\dQ_r$ and the rectification coefficient
\begin{equation}
\R=\frac{|\dQ_f|-|\dQ_r|}{|\dQ_f|+|\dQ_r|}.
\end{equation}
In the wide-band limit both responses take the Landauer form
\begin{equation}
\dQ_{f,r}=\int\frac{d\varepsilon}{2\pi}\,\varepsilon\,T_{RL,LR}(\varepsilon)\,[f_h(\varepsilon)-f_c(\varepsilon)],
\label{eq:landauer}
\end{equation}
with $f_{h,c}$ the Fermi functions at $T_{h,c}$.

\section{Exact rectification law}
\label{sec:law}

\subsection{Tridiagonal identity}

For a tridiagonal matrix $A$ of dimension $L$ with sub-diagonal elements $a_{j+1,j}$ and super-diagonal elements $a_{j,j+1}$, the corner elements of the inverse are
\begin{align}
(A^{-1})_{L1}&=\frac{(-1)^{L+1}}{\det A}\prod_{j=1}^{L-1}a_{j+1,j},\nonumber\\
(A^{-1})_{1L}&=\frac{(-1)^{L+1}}{\det A}\prod_{j=1}^{L-1}a_{j,j+1}.
\end{align}
Indeed, the minor obtained by deleting the first row and the last column of $A$ is triangular with the sub-diagonal of $A$ on its diagonal, and likewise for the other corner.
With $A=\varepsilon-\heff$ this gives
\begin{equation}
\frac{G_{L1}(\varepsilon)}{G_{1L}(\varepsilon)}=\prod_{j=1}^{L-1}\frac{(\heff)_{j+1,j}}{(\heff)_{j,j+1}}=\Bigl(\frac{t_-}{t_+}\Bigr)^{L-1}.
\label{eq:Gratio}
\end{equation}
The product involves no diagonal element, so it is independent of $\varepsilon$, of the on-site energies, of the end self-energies and of the uniform loss.
Hence
\begin{equation}
\frac{T_{RL}(\varepsilon)}{T_{LR}(\varepsilon)}=\frac{\dQ_f}{\dQ_r}=\Bigl|\frac{t_-}{t_+}\Bigr|^{2(L-1)}\equiv e^{2(L-1)/\xi},
\label{eq:law}
\end{equation}
and therefore $\R=\tanh[(L-1)/\xi]$.
The second equality holds because the ratio of the energy-resolved transmissions is a constant and the two thermal averages of Eq.~(\ref{eq:landauer}) share the same weight.

\subsection{Consequences}

Equation~(\ref{eq:law}) has four immediate consequences.
First, the ratio is the same for any pair of lead distributions, at any temperatures and chemical potentials and for any temperature difference, not only in linear response.
Second, the argument does not use the fermionic nature of the carriers: for bosons with the same bond loss the weight in Eq.~(\ref{eq:landauer}) is a difference of Bose functions and the ratio is unchanged.
Third, the ratio is independent of any on-site potential $\varepsilon_j$, random or not (Sec.~\ref{sec:disorder}).
Fourth, the law holds for the mesoscopic leads as well, since they modify only the end self-energies, which are diagonal.

Figure~\ref{fig1}(b) confirms Eq.~(\ref{eq:law}) to all digits for $L=5$--$160$ and $\kappa=0.003J$--$0.5J$, a scaling collapse of all lengths and loss rates onto one line.
Figure~\ref{fig1}(c) shows the design trade-off: $\R$ exceeds $0.99$ once $(L-1)/\xi>2.65$, i.e.\ $L/\xi\gtrsim3$, whereas the forward throughput decreases with $\kappa$ because the same loss that creates the skin effect removes energy from the chain; at $\kappa=0.07J$ and $L=40$ one has $\R=0.992$ with $\dQ_f$ equal to $49\%$ of its lossless value.
Equation~(\ref{eq:law}) is odd in $\phi$ and the rectification vanishes identically at $\phi=0,\pi$, where the dissipator is mirror symmetric.

\subsection{Same-chirality gain and the infinite-temperature bath}
\label{sec:gain}

Adding to every bond the gain operator $G_j=\sqrt{\kappa g}\,(c_j^\dagger+e^{-i\phi}c_{j+1}^\dagger)$ produces a matrix $M_g$ with the same elements as $M_l^{T}$, so $\heff$ is obtained from the pure-loss one by $\kappa\to\kappa(1+g)$: the law keeps its form with a shorter $\xi$.
Consequently a local bath at infinite temperature and zero chemical potential, implemented as loss $\kappa/2$ plus gain $\kappa/2$ of the same chirality, has exactly the free-fermion ratio of a pure loss $\kappa$.
We use this configuration in Sec.~\ref{sec:int} to test whether the occupation of the dissipator matters in the presence of interactions.

