\section{Beyond nearest neighbours: the non-Bloch exponent}
\label{sec:nonbloch}

Equation~(\ref{eq:law}) relies on tridiagonality.
Adding a reciprocal next-nearest-neighbour hopping $t_2\sum_j(c_j^\dagger c_{j+2}+{\rm h.c.})$ breaks it and makes the transmission asymmetry strongly energy dependent [Fig.~\ref{fig2}(a)]: it peaks near the lower band edge, decreases monotonically at high energy, and for $t_2\gtrsim0.3J$ even changes sign in a narrow window.

The non-Bloch band theory of the skin effect~\cite{Yao2018,Yokomizo2019} explains this quantitatively.
Writing bulk solutions of $\heff\psi=\varepsilon\psi$ as $\psi_j=\beta^j$ gives the characteristic equation
\begin{equation}
t_2\beta^2+t_+\beta-i\kappa-\varepsilon+t_-\beta^{-1}+t_2\beta^{-2}=0,
\label{eq:nonbloch}
\end{equation}
whose four roots, ordered as $|\beta_1|\le|\beta_2|\le|\beta_3|\le|\beta_4|$, satisfy $\beta_1\beta_2\beta_3\beta_4=1$ by Vieta's formula.
For a long open chain the transfer of amplitude between the two ends is dominated by the two roots of intermediate modulus, which form the generalised Brillouin zone when $|\beta_2|=|\beta_3|$; the amplitude transported from left to right scales as $|\beta_3|^{L}$ and that from right to left as $|\beta_2|^{-L}$, so that
\begin{equation}
\ln\frac{T_{RL}(\varepsilon)}{T_{LR}(\varepsilon)}=2(L-1)\ln|\beta_2(\varepsilon)\beta_3(\varepsilon)|+O(1).
\label{eq:nonblochlaw}
\end{equation}
For $t_2=0$ the equation is quadratic with $\beta_1\beta_2=t_-/t_+$, the $O(1)$ term vanishes identically and Eq.~(\ref{eq:law}) is recovered.
Equation~(\ref{eq:nonblochlaw}) reproduces the exact transmissions for all $t_2\le0.5J$ at $\kappa=0.1J$, $0.2J$ and $0.3J$ [dotted lines in Fig.~\ref{fig2}(a) for $\kappa=0.2J$].
The thermally averaged ratio therefore becomes the Fermi-window average of an energy-resolved skin length, $\xi(\varepsilon)^{-1}=\ln|\beta_2\beta_3|(\varepsilon)$, and it lies below the nearest-neighbour value [Fig.~\ref{fig2}(c)], for example $15.65\to8.11$ at $\kappa=0.2J$ when $t_2$ grows from $0$ to $0.5J$, while $\R$ stays above $0.97$.

This identifies the correct indicator of thermal nonreciprocity.
The spectral winding number around a reference energy is the usual topological marker of the skin effect~\cite{Gong2018,Okuma2020,Zhang2020}.
The periodic-boundary spectrum of $\heff$ for $\phi=\pi/2$ is
\begin{equation}
E(k)=-2J\cos k+2t_2\cos 2k-i\kappa(1-\sin k).
\end{equation}
Its imaginary part is non-positive and vanishes only at $k=\pi/2$, so the loop touches the real axis at one point [Fig.~\ref{fig2}(b)] and the winding number $w(\varepsilon)=(2\pi i)^{-1}\oint dk\,\partial_k\ln[E(k)-\varepsilon]$ is zero for every real $\varepsilon$ and every $t_2$.
The winding around the centre of the loop, $-i\kappa$, is $-1$ and signals the skin effect, but it is a single integer and cannot encode the energy dependence, the sign change and the $t_2$ dependence of the transmission asymmetry.
The chain rectifies although $w(\varepsilon)=0$: what matters is the radius ratio of the generalised Brillouin zone at the transport energy, not the winding of the spectrum.

