\section{Interacting chain}
\label{sec:int}

Does the law survive when quasiparticles scatter, given that interactions can create, reshape or destroy skin effects~\cite{Brighi2024,Koh2026,Roccati2026}?
We add the interaction $V\sum_j n_jn_{j+1}$ and compute the steady state of the full Lindbladian with matrix-product states (MPS).

\subsection{Superfermion matrix-product-state method}

The density matrix is vectorised in the superfermion representation~\cite{Prosen2008,Dzhioev2011}, $|\rho\rangle\!\rangle=\rho|I\rangle\!\rangle$ with $|I\rangle\!\rangle=\prod_j(1+c_j^\dagger\tilde c_j^\dagger)|0\rangle$, where $\tilde c_j$ are auxiliary fermions.
For a parity-even $\rho$ the rules $c_j\rho\to c_j$, $\rho c_j^\dagger\to-\tilde c_j$, $\rho c_j\to\tilde c_j^\dagger$ and $\rho c_i^\dagger c_j\to\tilde c_j^\dagger\tilde c_i$ map the Lindbladian onto a local operator on $2N$ fermionic modes, with $N=L+2K$; the bond dissipators $L_j\rho L_j^\dagger$ become bilinears on four modes and the interaction appears as $V n_jn_{j+1}-V\tilde n_j\tilde n_{j+1}$.
Each physical mode is grouped with its auxiliary partner into one MPS site of local dimension four, and the conserved charge $N-\tilde N=0$ is exploited.
Expectation values are $\langle\!\langle I|O|\rho\rangle\!\rangle/\langle\!\langle I|\rho\rangle\!\rangle$.
The lead jump operators commute with the interaction, so the lead energy currents are two-point functions and the current formulas of the Gaussian problem apply unchanged.

The $L$ chain sites and the $2K$ lead modes are arranged as [site 1][left lead][sites $2\dots L$][right lead], which keeps all long-range couplings anchored at a chain end and the bond dimension of the matrix-product operator of the Lindbladian at six.
The state is propagated with the second-order $W^{\rm II}$ matrix-product-operator exponential~\cite{Zaletel2015} of TeNPy~\cite{Hauschild2018} in steps $dt$, with singular-value compression at bond dimension $\chi$ and cutoff $10^{-12}$, starting from the product state with the exact Gaussian occupations, until the change of both lead currents over a time window of $2/J$ is below $10^{-5}J^2$ or $t=60/J$ is reached.
The mapping was verified exactly: on four modes with $V=0.7J$ the null vector of the vectorised Lindbladian coincides with the many-body steady state to $10^{-15}$, and for $V=0$, $L=6$, $K=4$ the propagated steady state agrees with the Lyapunov solution to $2\times10^{-3}$ in the currents.

\subsection{Precision and resolvable regime}

For $V=0$ the MPS result must coincide with the exact Gaussian solution, which calibrates the method.
The reverse response $\dQ_r$ is exponentially small and is the limiting quantity.
The truncation error of the absolute currents accumulates with the number of time steps, so it is not reduced by a smaller $dt$; it is reduced only by $\chi$.
Table~\ref{tab:chi} lists the free-fermion calibration at $\kappa=0.05J$, $L=16$.
At $\chi=256$ and $dt=0.05/J$ the logarithmic ratio is reproduced within $0.003$ and $\dQ_r$ within $0.03\%$; this setting is used for all production runs.
At $\kappa=0.1J$ the residual bias of $\ln(\dQ_f/\dQ_r)$ at $\chi=256$ is $+0.045$ ($L=12$) and $-0.038$ ($L=16$), which we quote as the error bar; slopes at this $\kappa$ are extracted from the control-variate estimate $\ln r_{\rm cv}(V)=\ln r_{\rm MPS}(V)-\ln r_{\rm MPS}(0)+\ln r_{\rm exact}(0)$, with $r=\dQ_f/\dQ_r$, which cancels the common truncation bias.
The time fluctuation over the last five windows contributes less than $0.01$ to $\ln(\dQ_f/\dQ_r)$, except for the infinite-temperature-bath run at $V=2J$, where it is $0.05$.

