$dt\,J$ & 0.1 & 0.05 & 0.025 & 0.05 \\
\colrule
$\ln(\dQ_f/\dQ_r)$ & 1.347 & 1.412 & 1.462 & 1.5033 \\
$\dQ_r$ bias & $+16.8\%$ & $+7.7\%$ & $+2.6\%$ & $+0.03\%$ \\
\end{tabular}
\end{ruledtabular}
\end{table}

The floor of the absolute currents is about $10^{-3}J^2$ at $\chi=128$ and two orders of magnitude lower at $\chi=256$.
The reverse response can therefore be resolved only when $\dQ_r/\dQ_f=e^{-2(L-1)/\xi}$ is not much smaller than $10^{-2}$, i.e.\ $2(L-1)\kappa/J\lesssim4$.
We work at $\kappa=0.03J$--$0.1J$ and $L=12$--$20$, and use the slope of $\ln(\dQ_f/\dQ_r)$ against $L$ as the observable that measures $2/\xi_{\rm eff}$.
For a stronger skin effect ($\kappa=0.2J$, $L=20$--$40$) the simulations yield only lower bounds, $\R\ge0.85$--$0.98$ for all $V\le2J$, together with the forward throughput, which decreases monotonically with $V$ (from $1.10$ to $0.63\times10^{-2}J^2$ at $L=20$ and from $0.70$ to $0.33\times10^{-2}J^2$ at $L=40$ for $V=0\to2J$).

\subsection{Results}

Figure~\ref{fig3} and Table~\ref{tab:int} show that the interaction acts as a pure prefactor.
At $\kappa=0.05J$ and $V=2J$ the slope is $0.0975$ against the free-fermion $0.1000$, and the offset $\Delta_V=\ln(\dQ_f/\dQ_r)|_{V=2J}-\ln(\dQ_f/\dQ_r)|_{V=0}$ is $-0.42$ at both $L=16$ and $L=20$; at $\kappa=0.1J$ the slope is $0.207$ against $0.200$ and $\Delta_V=-0.54$ and $-0.52$ at $L=12$ and $16$.
The $\chi=128$ series over $L=12,16,20$ give slopes $2/\xi_{\rm eff}=0.055$, $0.066$, $0.046$, $0.043$ ($\pm0.013$; exact $0.060$) at $\kappa=0.03J$ and $0.096$, $0.101$, $0.125$, $0.094$ ($\pm0.019$; exact $0.100$) at $\kappa=0.05J$ for $V=0$, $0.5J$, $J$, $2J$, consistent with $2/\xi$ for all $V$ within their $20\%$ uncertainty [Fig.~\ref{fig3}(c)].
Hence $\xi$ is not renormalised by $V$ up to $2J$, to within $\pm2.5\%$ at $\kappa=0.05J$: the exponential structure $e^{2(L-1)/\xi}$ of Eq.~(\ref{eq:law}) is a property of the dissipator, and the interaction only reduces both responses, the forward one by $25$--$35\%$ at $V=2J$ [Fig.~\ref{fig3}(d)] and the reverse one less, which produces the negative offset.

The offset does not depend on the occupation of the dissipator either.
Replacing the pure loss by the local infinite-temperature bath of Sec.~\ref{sec:gain} with the same total rate gives $\Delta_V=-0.54$ at $\kappa=0.1J$, $L=12$, identical to the pure-loss value within $0.002$ [green symbols in Fig.~\ref{fig3}(b)].
The exact invariance of the free-fermion ratio under same-chirality gain thus persists numerically in the interacting chain.
The $V=0$ bias of the MPS is an order of magnitude smaller for the infinite-temperature bath ($+0.001$ against $+0.045$), because the steady state is closer to the maximally mixed state and carries less operator entanglement.

