\section{Thermodynamic cost}
\label{sec:thermo}

\subsection{Finite-temperature bulk bath}

A pure loss channel is a reservoir at zero temperature and infinitely negative chemical potential, and the entropy balance of the diode is then ill defined.
To close it we replace each $L_j$ by a mesoscopic bath: bond $j$ is coupled to $K_b$ bath modes with energies in $[-W_b,W_b]$, thermalised at $(T_b,\mu_b)$ in the same way as the lead modes, through the bond operator $A_j=c_j+e^{i\phi}c_{j+1}$ with amplitude $v_b=\sqrt{\kappa\Delta\varepsilon_b/2\pi}$.
The damping rate of $A_j$ is then $\kappa$, detailed balance holds, and the pure-loss model is recovered for $\mu_b\to-\infty$.
All reservoirs now have a temperature, and the entropy production
\begin{equation}
\sigma=-\sum_\alpha\frac{\dot E_\alpha-\mu_\alpha\dot N_\alpha}{T_\alpha}\ge0,
\label{eq:sigma}
\end{equation}
where $\dot E_\alpha$ and $\dot N_\alpha$ are the energy and particle currents from reservoir $\alpha$ into the system, is defined; the model remains quadratic and is solved with Eq.~(\ref{eq:lyap}).
We verified $\sigma\ge0$ in all configurations and $\sigma=0$ to $10^{-8}$ when all reservoirs share one temperature.
We use $L=40$, $K=10$ lead modes, $K_b=10$ and $W_b=4J$.

\subsection{The ratio is independent of the bath state}

The ratio $\dQ_f/\dQ_r$ is exactly independent of $(T_b,\mu_b)$, because both responses of Eq.~(\ref{eq:dQ}) are linear in the lead distributions and the bath contribution cancels in the difference; numerically the ratio agrees to $10^{-9}$ for $T_b=0.25J$--$2J$ and $\mu_b=0$ to $-30J$ [Fig.~\ref{fig4}(b)].
The dissipator fixes the nonreciprocity; the occupation of the bath only fixes its cost.

Because the self-energy of a discrete bath is energy dependent, the effective amplitudes $t_\pm(\varepsilon)$ are not constant and the ratio falls slightly below the wide-band law: at $\kappa=0.2J$ we obtain $\ln(\dQ_f/\dQ_r)=14.23$, $14.48$, $14.68$ and $14.95$ for $(K_b,W_b/J)=(10,4)$, $(10,6)$, $(20,4)$ or $(20,6)$, and $(40,6)$, approaching the wide-band value $15.65$.
The Lindblad loss of Eq.~(\ref{eq:Lj}) is the exact wide-band limit.

\subsection{Entropy production per rectified heat}

With all baths at the cold temperature and $\mu_b=0$ the isothermal state produces no entropy, so the entropy production $\sigma_f$ of the forward configuration is that of the rectification process itself.
It is nearly independent of $\kappa$: $\sigma_f=0.042$, $0.044$, $0.047$, $0.049$ and $0.052\,J$ for $\kappa=0.05J$, $0.1J$, $0.2J$, $0.3J$ and $0.5J$, whereas $\dQ_f=1.64$, $1.11$, $0.66$, $0.47$ and $0.29\times10^{-2}J^2$.
The entropy produced per unit of rectified heat therefore grows as
\begin{equation}
\frac{\sigma_f}{\dQ_f}\propto\kappa\propto\frac{1}{\xi},
\end{equation}
taking the values $2.6$, $4.0$, $7.0$, $10.4$ and $18.1\,J^{-1}$ [Fig.~\ref{fig4}(a)].
The cost is carried by the heat that leaks from the hot lead into the bulk bath, which grows from $0.026$ to $0.049\,J^2$ over the same range.

The diode works at half filling with no particle current when $\mu_b=0$.
Operating it as a particle sink drains the chain and is thermodynamically expensive: for $\kappa=0.2J$ the sweep $\mu_b=0$, $-0.5J$, $-J$, $-2J$, $-4J$, $-30J$ gives $\sigma_f=0.047$, $0.12$, $0.34$, $1.15$, $3.2$ and $25.5\,J$ and mean fillings $0.50$, $0.44$, $0.38$, $0.25$, $0.12$ and $0.10$ for the same ratio [Fig.~\ref{fig4}(b)]; the skin-like density pile-up at the edge appears only for $\mu_b<0$.
For $T_b\neq T_c$ the isothermal state already produces entropy through heat flow between bath and leads, and the difference $\sigma_f-\sigma_{\rm iso}$ can be negative; we therefore quote the cost at $T_b=T_c$.

\subsection{Mirror identity for the entropy production}

We find the exact identity
\begin{equation}
\sigma_f(T_b=T_c)=\sigma_f(T_b=T_h),
\label{eq:mirror}
\end{equation}
which holds for any $\phi$, $\kappa$ and $t_2$ and has a simple origin.
Let $P$ be the site reflection $j\to L+1-j$.
For the uniform chiral chain $P\heff P$ exchanges $t_+$ and $t_-$, which is also what transposition does, so $P\heff P=\heff^{T}$ and $PGP=G^{T}$.
The two end elements of the Green function therefore coincide, $G_{LL}=(PGP)_{11}=(G^{T})_{11}=G_{11}$, and so do the reflection probabilities $R_{LL}(\varepsilon)=R_{RR}(\varepsilon)$ of the two leads, although $G_{L1}\neq G_{1L}$.
In the multi-terminal Landauer form with all reservoirs thermal, the entropy production with every reservoir except the left lead at $T_c$ is
\begin{equation}
\sigma=\Bigl(\frac{1}{T_c}-\frac{1}{T_h}\Bigr)\int\frac{d\varepsilon}{2\pi}\,\varepsilon\,[f_h-f_c]\,[1-R_{LL}(\varepsilon)],
\end{equation}
and with every reservoir except the right lead at $T_h$ it is the same expression with $R_{RR}$, where the sum rules $\sum_\beta T_{\alpha\beta}=\sum_\beta T_{\beta\alpha}=1-R_{\alpha\alpha}$ for the transmissions $T_{\alpha\beta}$ between reservoirs have been used.
Equal reflectances give Eq.~(\ref{eq:mirror}).
Numerically the two sides agree to $10^{-12}$ for the clean chain; on-site disorder breaks the identity (difference $3\times10^{-3}$ for $W_d=J$) and mirror-symmetric disorder restores it ($10^{-12}$).
Physically, the heat emitted by a hot lead into an otherwise cold device equals the heat absorbed by a cold lead from an otherwise hot device: nonreciprocity appears in transmission but not in reflection.

\section{Disorder}
\label{sec:disorder}

\subsection{On-site disorder}

Because Eq.~(\ref{eq:Gratio}) involves no diagonal element of $\heff$, on-site disorder of any strength and distribution leaves $T_{RL}(\varepsilon)/T_{LR}(\varepsilon)$, $\dQ_f/\dQ_r$ and $\R$ exactly unchanged, sample by sample.
For $\varepsilon_j$ uniform in $[-W_d/2,W_d/2]$, $L=40$ and wide-band leads, the rectification is sample independent to $10^{-14}$: $\R=0.9604$, $0.9992$ and $1.0000$ for $\kappa=0.05J$, $0.1J$ and $0.2J$ at every $W_d$ [Fig.~\ref{fig4}(c)].
The throughput, in contrast, collapses by Anderson localisation: at $\kappa=0.1J$ the median $\dQ_f$ is $1.62$, $1.26$, $0.61$, $0.051$, $0.0014$ and $0.00016\times10^{-2}J^2$ for $W_d=0$, $0.5J$, $J$, $2J$, $3J$ and $4J$.
The rectification is thus independent of eigenstate localisation, which affects both directions equally; the only practical requirement is a usable throughput, $W_d\lesssim2J$ for $\dQ_f\gtrsim10^{-3}J^2$.

Table~\ref{tab:decay} lists the forward decay rate $-d\langle\ln\dQ_f\rangle/dL$ between $L=10$ and $80$.
In the weak-disorder regime the loss-induced and Anderson decay rates are nearly additive.
The skin effect does not delocalise the forward direction, which is attenuated by loss and localisation alike; it fixes the ratio of the two directions and nothing else.

