\subsection{Bond disorder and the sum rule}

With bond-dependent $\kappa_j$ and $\phi_j$ the hopping amplitudes are $t_{\mp,j}=-J-\tfrac{i\kappa_j}{2}e^{\mp i\phi_j}$ and Eq.~(\ref{eq:Gratio}) gives the sum rule
\begin{equation}
\ln\frac{\dQ_f}{\dQ_r}=2\sum_{j=1}^{L-1}\ln\Bigl|\frac{t_{-,j}}{t_{+,j}}\Bigr|,
\label{eq:sumrule}
\end{equation}
which we verified to $10^{-14}$ on $10^2$ random samples with on-site disorder present at the same time.
The exponent is a sum of independent bond contributions: it is self-averaging, with sample-to-sample fluctuations $\propto\sqrt{L}$ and a relative width that vanishes for long chains [Fig.~\ref{fig4}(d)].
At $\kappa=0.1J$ a spread of loss rates, $\kappa_j=\kappa(1\pm0.8u)$ with $u$ uniform in $[0,1]$, leaves the mean unchanged ($3.75$, $7.85$ and $15.83$ against the clean $3.80$, $7.81$ and $15.81$ for $L=20$, $40$ and $80$) with standard deviations $0.43$, $0.55$ and $0.88$.
A spread of phases, $\phi_j=\pi/2\pm1$, rescales the mean by $\langle\sin\phi_j\rangle=\sin1\simeq0.84$ ($6.56$ against $7.81\times0.84=6.57$ for $L=40$) with standard deviations $0.13$--$0.25$.
Only random chirality, $\phi_j=\pm\pi/2$ with equal probability, destroys the rectification on average: the means are $-0.07$, $-0.04$ and $0.16$ and the standard deviations $0.91$, $1.34$ and $1.77$, so the rectification direction is random from sample to sample.

\subsection{Disorder beyond nearest neighbours}

With $t_2\neq0$ the on-site invariance is lost and the ratio becomes sample dependent, but only weakly: for $t_2=0.3J$ and $\kappa=0.2J$ we find $\ln(\dQ_f/\dQ_r)=10.12\pm0.11$, $9.71\pm0.19$ and $9.42\pm0.30$ for $W_d=0.5J$, $J$ and $2J$, against the clean value $10.31$.

\section{Experimental realisation}
\label{sec:exp}

The bond loss of Eq.~(\ref{eq:Lj}) follows from coupling neighbouring sites to a strongly damped auxiliary mode $a_j$, $g(c_j^\dagger a_j+e^{i\phi}c_{j+1}^\dagger a_j+{\rm h.c.})$, with decay rate $\Gamma_a\gg g$, giving $\kappa=4g^2/\Gamma_a$.
Table~\ref{tab:exp} summarises parameter sets for two platforms; the requirement $\R>0.99$ corresponds to $(L-1)/\xi>2.65$ with $\xi\simeq J/\kappa$ at $\phi=\pi/2$.

In momentum-lattice Bose condensates a dissipative Aharonov--Bohm chain of exactly this type has produced the non-Hermitian skin effect~\cite{Liang2022}; with $J=2\pi\times0.5$~kHz, $\kappa/J=0.05$--$0.2$ requires $\kappa=2\pi\times25$--$100$~Hz, and $\R>0.99$ needs $L\simeq15$ sites at $\kappa/J=0.2$.
Thermal leads are provided by the two-terminal geometry of cold-atom transport~\cite{Brantut2012,Krinner2017}, and $\dQ_{f,r}$ follow from the energy and atom-number changes of the reservoirs.

In superconducting circuits ($J=2\pi\times10$--$50$~MHz) a low-$Q$ auxiliary resonator per bond with $\Gamma_a\sim2\pi\times10$~MHz and $g\sim2\pi\times1$--$3$~MHz gives $\kappa=2\pi\times0.5$--$2$~MHz, with thermal leads realised by noise temperatures of $20$--$300$~mK injected into the end transmission lines.
The carriers are then photons, but Eq.~(\ref{eq:law}) is an identity of single-particle Green functions and holds for bosons with pure loss as well (Sec.~\ref{sec:law}).
Skin effects have so far been reached in superconducting hardware through squeezing in multimode cavities~\cite{Busnaina2024} and by post-selection on digital processors~\cite{Shen2025}; a loss-engineered Liouvillian skin effect in a circuit has not been reported, although its photonic counterpart has~\cite{Yang2026}, and the heat diode proposed here is a natural first target for one.
Both platforms tolerate the site-to-site frequency disorder that is unavoidable in practice, because it does not enter Eq.~(\ref{eq:law}).

