\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We have shown that a reciprocal chain with directional bond dissipation is a heat diode whose rectification ratio is fixed by one number, the Liouvillian skin length, through an exact exponential law, and we have mapped the law's domain of validity in four directions: beyond nearest neighbours, with interactions, with a thermal dissipator and with disorder.

Two trends organise the results.
The exponent is a transport property of the dissipator: it is the non-Bloch localisation length at the transport energy, it is blind to the spectral winding number, to on-site disorder and to the bath occupation, and it is not renormalised by interactions up to $V=2J$.
In contrast, every mechanism that makes the device less ideal acts on the prefactor: interactions, next-nearest-neighbour hopping and Anderson localisation all lower the throughput and never the slope.
Quantitatively, the ratio grows as $e^{2(L-1)/\xi}$ and reaches $\R>0.99$ at $L/\xi\simeq3$, in contrast to the size-independent ratios of nonlinear thermal rectifiers~\cite{Li2012}, and the price is an entropy production per rectified heat of order $\kappa$.

These results place skin-effect transport in the same category as topological edge transport: a directional response set by a bulk length rather than by local details, with disorder affecting only the magnitude.
They also carry a warning for experiments on dissipative lattices: a heat or particle current asymmetry that is not measured as a function of length cannot be attributed to the skin effect, because the prefactor is model dependent while only the exponent is universal, and the entropy budget must be measured against the bath occupation and not against the loss rate alone.
Two questions remain open: whether the slope stays unrenormalised for interactions strong enough to drive the chain into a correlated insulator, and whether the identity of Eq.~(\ref{eq:mirror}) reflects a fluctuation relation for Liouvillian skin systems.
Engineered dissipation is now routine in cold atoms and circuits, and the law derived here gives a direct, size-resolved test of whether a dissipative lattice rectifies heat for the right reason.

